COLD CALL SCRIPT — {{BUSINESS_NAME}}

Goal of this call: get permission to send the audit findings. Not to
sell on the spot, not to close anything. A confirmed email address is a
full win.

---

OPENER (10-15 sec — get past the reflex to hang up)

"Hi, is this {{CONTACT_NAME}}? ... Hey, this is {{YOUR_NAME}} — I'm not
selling anything today, I actually just ran a quick technical scan on
your website and found something worth a 30-second heads up. Got a sec?"

If they're rushed or say no: "No worries — can I just shoot you a quick
email with what I found instead?" Get the email, thank them, hang up
warmly. Always leave the door open — a "call back later" is a fine
outcome too, just don't push past it.

---

THE HOOK (lead with the specific finding, not a generic pitch)

"So here's the thing — {{TOP_ISSUE_PLAIN_LANGUAGE}}. That's
{{PLAIN_LANGUAGE_CONSEQUENCE}}."

Never state a score or number that isn't the real one from the audit.
Never invent urgency ("your competitors are already ahead of you")
unless it's something the audit actually found — a real finding is
more persuasive than manufactured pressure anyway.

---

THE OFFER

"I put together a short breakdown — happy to send it over, no charge,
no obligation. Want me to send it to {{EMAIL}}?"

If yes: confirm the email back to them, say when they'll get it
(today/tomorrow), end the call. Don't linger once you have the yes.

---

COMMON OBJECTIONS

- "We already have someone doing our SEO."
  → "Totally fair — this is just a second look at the technical side,
    the kind of thing worth a gut-check either way. I'll send it over
    and you can compare notes with them."

- "How much does this cost?"
  → "The report itself is free — I wanted to flag something concrete
    instead of a generic pitch. If it's useful and you want help
    acting on it, we can talk about that after you've seen it."

- "Not interested."
  → "No problem at all — appreciate your time." Don't push, don't
    re-pitch. Log it and move on.

- "Can you just email me?"
  → "Of course — what's the best address?" This is a win, not a
    deflection — it was the actual goal of the call.

- "How'd you get my number / is this a scam?"
  → Be straightforward about how you found them (their public site, a
    business directory). Never claim a referral or relationship that
    doesn't exist.

---

VOICEMAIL (don't just hang up if it goes to voicemail)

"Hi {{CONTACT_NAME}}, this is {{YOUR_NAME}}. I ran a quick technical
check on {{BUSINESS_NAME}}'s website and found something worth a quick
look — nothing scary, just something concrete. I'll follow up with an
email so you've got it in writing either way. Talk soon."

---

THE CLOSE

Every call ends one of two ways: an email address confirmed, or a
clean "not now" logged so there's no re-calling too soon. Both are a
fine outcome — the only bad outcome is being pushy about it.

